    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay takes a comparative historical approach to the two technologies –– nuclear and Artificial Intelligence -- to examine the contextual developments and political-philosophical ramifications of each technology. The author urges us to see past the polarized conversation between “AI doomsayers” and “AI optimists” and learn from the nuclear events of the past before a “potentially catastrophic second dance with the apocalypse.”",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sato, L. (2024). Not with a Bang But a Computer: An Investigation in Promoting Safe AI Research Based on Lessons Learned from the History of Nuclear Technology Development. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/st462ff9789. https://doi.org/10.25740/st462ff9789.",
